1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,280
Why does a 3 p.m.

2
00:00:01,280 --> 00:00:03,520
coffee still cost you sleep at midnight,

3
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:05,440
when you feel completely fine?

4
00:00:05,440 --> 00:00:06,880
You drank it hours ago.

5
00:00:06,880 --> 00:00:08,720
The mug is already washed and back

6
00:00:08,720 --> 00:00:09,520
in the cupboard.

7
00:00:09,520 --> 00:00:10,720
You don't feel wired,

8
00:00:10,720 --> 00:00:12,320
you climb into bed on time,

9
00:00:12,320 --> 00:00:13,840
and you drift off more or less

10
00:00:13,840 --> 00:00:14,560
as usual.

11
00:00:14,560 --> 00:00:15,280
In the morning,

12
00:00:15,280 --> 00:00:16,720
you'd swear you slept fine.

13
00:00:16,720 --> 00:00:18,800
But a sleep monitor might disagree.

14
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:20,240
Here's the thing most people get

15
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:21,120
backwards:

16
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:23,440
caffeine doesn't actually give you energy.

17
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:25,120
It blocks the message that says you're

18
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:25,680
tired.

19
00:00:25,680 --> 00:00:27,760
And that message doesn't go away just

20
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:29,200
because you stopped hearing it.

21
00:00:29,200 --> 00:00:31,280
In this video we'll meet the messenger,

22
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:32,800
the bean that blocks it,

23
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:34,560
and the clock that decides how long

24
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:35,620
it stays blocked.

25
00:00:35,620 --> 00:00:37,460
So let's follow the message,

26
00:00:37,460 --> 00:00:39,140
from the moment it's written to the

27
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:41,140
moment it finally gets delivered.

28
00:00:41,140 --> 00:00:42,180
For a 3 p.m.

29
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:42,900
coffee,

30
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:44,420
that can be well after you've gone

31
00:00:44,420 --> 00:00:44,980
to bed.

32
00:00:44,980 --> 00:00:46,420
Every hour you're awake,

33
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:48,820
your brain cells burn through energy,

34
00:00:48,820 --> 00:00:50,340
and one of the leftovers is a

35
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:52,180
molecule called adenosine.

36
00:00:52,180 --> 00:00:54,340
Think of adenosine as a mail carrier

37
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:55,940
with one letter to deliver,

38
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:56,980
over and over.

39
00:00:56,980 --> 00:00:58,420
The letter says one word:

40
00:00:58,420 --> 00:00:59,140
tired.

41
00:00:59,140 --> 00:01:00,260
No return address,

42
00:01:00,260 --> 00:01:01,300
no small talk.

43
00:01:01,300 --> 00:01:03,380
Your brain has mailboxes built just for

44
00:01:03,380 --> 00:01:04,340
those letters,

45
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:06,420
called adenosine receptors.

46
00:01:06,420 --> 00:01:08,340
Every time a letter drops in,

47
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:09,700
the parts of your brain that keep

48
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:11,300
you alert slow down a little.

49
00:01:11,300 --> 00:01:12,500
The longer you're awake,

50
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:13,784
the more letters arrive.

51
00:01:13,784 --> 00:01:15,224
By the end of a long day,

52
00:01:15,224 --> 00:01:17,144
the mail carrier is shoving them through

53
00:01:17,144 --> 00:01:18,184
with both hands.

54
00:01:18,184 --> 00:01:18,824
That heavy,

55
00:01:18,824 --> 00:01:21,064
yawning feeling is what scientists call

56
00:01:21,064 --> 00:01:21,944
sleep pressure.

57
00:01:21,944 --> 00:01:24,824
Adenosine isn't the only thing behind it,

58
00:01:24,824 --> 00:01:26,424
but scientists think it's one of the

59
00:01:26,424 --> 00:01:27,144
big ones.

60
00:01:27,144 --> 00:01:28,024
Then you sleep,

61
00:01:28,024 --> 00:01:30,104
the letters get cleared away overnight,

62
00:01:30,104 --> 00:01:32,264
and you wake up with empty mailboxes.

63
00:01:32,264 --> 00:01:32,984
Sleep is,

64
00:01:32,984 --> 00:01:34,024
among other things,

65
00:01:34,024 --> 00:01:35,704
your brain doing its paperwork.

66
00:01:35,704 --> 00:01:37,224
It's a tidy system,

67
00:01:37,224 --> 00:01:38,984
right up until someone tampers with the

68
00:01:38,984 --> 00:01:39,464
mail.

69
00:01:39,464 --> 00:01:40,664
Enter caffeine.

70
00:01:40,664 --> 00:01:42,504
About fifteen to thirty minutes after your

71
00:01:42,504 --> 00:01:43,384
first sip,

72
00:01:43,384 --> 00:01:44,584
it starts to kick in.

73
00:01:44,584 --> 00:01:46,504
Caffeine happens to be shaped a lot

74
00:01:46,504 --> 00:01:47,784
like adenosine.

75
00:01:47,784 --> 00:01:49,624
Close enough to slide right into the

76
00:01:49,624 --> 00:01:50,664
mailbox slot.

77
00:01:50,664 --> 00:01:51,704
But it isn't a letter,

78
00:01:51,704 --> 00:01:53,341
so it doesn't deliver anything.

79
00:01:53,341 --> 00:01:54,541
It just sits there,

80
00:01:54,541 --> 00:01:56,381
wearing a sign that says closed.

81
00:01:56,381 --> 00:01:58,941
Now the tiredness letters arrive and find

82
00:01:58,941 --> 00:01:59,981
the slot taken.

83
00:01:59,981 --> 00:02:01,101
They can't get in,

84
00:02:01,101 --> 00:02:02,861
so your brain never gets the message

85
00:02:02,861 --> 00:02:03,821
that you're tired.

86
00:02:03,821 --> 00:02:05,261
It checks the mailbox,

87
00:02:05,261 --> 00:02:06,541
finds nothing new,

88
00:02:06,541 --> 00:02:08,221
and decides you must be fine.

89
00:02:08,221 --> 00:02:09,421
That's the whole trick.

90
00:02:09,421 --> 00:02:11,261
Caffeine doesn't hand you fuel;

91
00:02:11,261 --> 00:02:13,021
a cup of black coffee has about

92
00:02:13,021 --> 00:02:14,061
two calories.

93
00:02:14,061 --> 00:02:15,661
It just stops your brain from reading

94
00:02:15,661 --> 00:02:16,621
the bad news.

95
00:02:16,621 --> 00:02:18,061
At everyday doses,

96
00:02:18,061 --> 00:02:20,141
blocking adenosine is the main thing

97
00:02:20,141 --> 00:02:21,821
caffeine does in your brain.

98
00:02:21,821 --> 00:02:22,461
Meanwhile,

99
00:02:22,461 --> 00:02:23,421
you're still awake,

100
00:02:23,421 --> 00:02:25,341
so adenosine keeps being made.

101
00:02:25,341 --> 00:02:26,781
The mail carrier knocks,

102
00:02:26,781 --> 00:02:27,421
waits,

103
00:02:27,421 --> 00:02:29,261
and leaves another letter on the step.

104
00:02:29,261 --> 00:02:30,701
The letters don't vanish;

105
00:02:30,701 --> 00:02:32,240
they stack up outside the door.

106
00:02:32,240 --> 00:02:34,640
Caffeine doesn't cancel your tiredness,

107
00:02:34,640 --> 00:02:35,840
it puts it on hold.

108
00:02:35,840 --> 00:02:38,000
And everything on hold gets delivered

109
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:38,720
eventually.

110
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:40,400
So how long does caffeine stay in

111
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:41,120
the slot?

112
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:42,400
That's up to your liver,

113
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:44,240
which slowly breaks caffeine down and

114
00:02:44,240 --> 00:02:45,200
clears it out.

115
00:02:45,200 --> 00:02:47,760
Scientists measure this with a half-life:

116
00:02:47,760 --> 00:02:49,200
the time it takes your body to

117
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:50,240
get rid of half of it.

118
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:52,000
For a typical healthy adult,

119
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:54,240
caffeine's half-life is around four to

120
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:55,600
five hours on average.

121
00:02:55,600 --> 00:02:57,360
That's a long time for something you

122
00:02:57,360 --> 00:02:58,720
finished in ten minutes.

123
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:00,320
And that average hides a lot of

124
00:03:00,320 --> 00:03:01,040
wobble.

125
00:03:01,040 --> 00:03:02,560
Some people clear half of it in

126
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:03,520
about two hours.

127
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:04,240
For others,

128
00:03:04,240 --> 00:03:05,760
it takes eight hours or more.

129
00:03:05,760 --> 00:03:07,520
Your speed depends on things like your

130
00:03:07,520 --> 00:03:08,160
genes,

131
00:03:08,160 --> 00:03:08,960
your age,

132
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:10,000
whether you smoke,

133
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:11,154
and even your diet.

134
00:03:11,154 --> 00:03:13,634
Pregnancy and some medicines can slow it

135
00:03:13,634 --> 00:03:14,354
down too.

136
00:03:14,354 --> 00:03:15,154
If that's you,

137
00:03:15,154 --> 00:03:17,154
the person to ask is your doctor,

138
00:03:17,154 --> 00:03:18,514
not a cartoon bean.

139
00:03:18,514 --> 00:03:19,154
Either way,

140
00:03:19,154 --> 00:03:20,994
caffeine never leaves all at once.

141
00:03:20,994 --> 00:03:22,194
After one half-life,

142
00:03:22,194 --> 00:03:23,394
half of it is gone.

143
00:03:23,394 --> 00:03:24,194
After another,

144
00:03:24,194 --> 00:03:25,394
half of what's left.

145
00:03:25,394 --> 00:03:26,914
It leaves the way a guest leaves

146
00:03:26,914 --> 00:03:27,714
a party:

147
00:03:27,714 --> 00:03:28,514
slowly,

148
00:03:28,514 --> 00:03:30,514
and saying goodbye several times.

149
00:03:30,514 --> 00:03:32,274
Let's run the numbers on that 3

150
00:03:32,274 --> 00:03:32,594
p.m.

151
00:03:32,594 --> 00:03:33,234
coffee.

152
00:03:33,234 --> 00:03:34,807
Say it's a small cup with about

153
00:03:34,807 --> 00:03:36,674
a hundred milligrams of caffeine.

154
00:03:36,674 --> 00:03:37,794
Cups vary a lot,

155
00:03:37,794 --> 00:03:39,554
so this is just a round number.

156
00:03:39,554 --> 00:03:41,714
And say your half-life is five hours,

157
00:03:41,714 --> 00:03:43,154
near the middle of the range.

158
00:03:43,154 --> 00:03:44,754
Here's what's still in you as the

159
00:03:44,754 --> 00:03:45,794
evening goes on.

160
00:03:45,794 --> 00:03:46,994
By 8 p.m.,

161
00:03:46,994 --> 00:03:49,097
about fifty milligrams are still on duty.

162
00:03:49,097 --> 00:03:49,897
By midnight,

163
00:03:49,897 --> 00:03:51,577
nine hours after the cup,

164
00:03:51,577 --> 00:03:53,337
you've still got roughly thirty.

165
00:03:53,337 --> 00:03:54,537
That's more than a quarter of the

166
00:03:54,537 --> 00:03:55,017
cup,

167
00:03:55,017 --> 00:03:56,937
sitting in your mailboxes while you try

168
00:03:56,937 --> 00:03:57,577
to sleep.

169
00:03:57,577 --> 00:03:59,337
If your half-life is closer to eight

170
00:03:59,337 --> 00:03:59,897
hours,

171
00:03:59,897 --> 00:04:01,177
it's nearer to half.

172
00:04:01,177 --> 00:04:02,697
And if you had a big coffee-shop

173
00:04:02,697 --> 00:04:03,177
cup,

174
00:04:03,177 --> 00:04:04,537
start with a bigger number.

175
00:04:04,537 --> 00:04:06,537
You probably won't feel thirty milligrams

176
00:04:06,537 --> 00:04:07,337
as a buzz.

177
00:04:07,337 --> 00:04:08,777
But it doesn't need to buzz to

178
00:04:08,777 --> 00:04:10,217
keep a few slots closed.

179
00:04:10,217 --> 00:04:12,217
The closed signs are smaller now,

180
00:04:12,217 --> 00:04:13,817
but they're still up at bedtime.

181
00:04:13,817 --> 00:04:15,017
And at 7 a.m.,

182
00:04:15,017 --> 00:04:16,377
sixteen hours later,

183
00:04:16,377 --> 00:04:18,617
there's still about ten milligrams left.

184
00:04:18,617 --> 00:04:20,457
Just in time for the next coffee.

185
00:04:20,457 --> 00:04:22,617
So does that leftover caffeine actually

186
00:04:22,617 --> 00:04:23,737
cost you sleep?

187
00:04:23,737 --> 00:04:25,417
In 2013,

188
00:04:25,417 --> 00:04:27,657
researchers led by Christopher Drake

189
00:04:27,657 --> 00:04:28,869
tested exactly that.

190
00:04:28,869 --> 00:04:30,949
Twelve healthy sleepers took a caffeine

191
00:04:30,949 --> 00:04:31,909
pill at bedtime,

192
00:04:31,909 --> 00:04:33,749
three hours before bed,

193
00:04:33,749 --> 00:04:35,429
or six hours before bed.

194
00:04:35,429 --> 00:04:37,269
Each of them tried every version,

195
00:04:37,269 --> 00:04:38,629
plus a dummy pill,

196
00:04:38,629 --> 00:04:40,629
and nobody knew which night was which.

197
00:04:40,629 --> 00:04:42,069
They went to bed at their usual

198
00:04:42,069 --> 00:04:42,629
time,

199
00:04:42,629 --> 00:04:43,349
at home,

200
00:04:43,349 --> 00:04:45,189
wearing a headband that recorded their

201
00:04:45,189 --> 00:04:46,149
brain waves.

202
00:04:46,149 --> 00:04:47,349
The dose was big:

203
00:04:47,349 --> 00:04:48,869
400 milligrams,

204
00:04:48,869 --> 00:04:50,869
roughly four small cups of coffee.

205
00:04:50,869 --> 00:04:52,789
That's the most the FDA considers

206
00:04:52,789 --> 00:04:54,629
generally fine for a whole day,

207
00:04:54,629 --> 00:04:55,829
in a single pill.

208
00:04:55,829 --> 00:04:57,589
Even when the pill came six hours

209
00:04:57,589 --> 00:04:58,469
before bed,

210
00:04:58,469 --> 00:04:59,909
they lost more than an hour of

211
00:04:59,909 --> 00:05:00,469
sleep.

212
00:05:00,469 --> 00:05:02,069
And here's the strange part.

213
00:05:02,069 --> 00:05:03,749
In their own sleep diaries,

214
00:05:03,749 --> 00:05:05,749
the six-hour nights didn't clearly stand

215
00:05:05,749 --> 00:05:06,149
out.

216
00:05:06,149 --> 00:05:07,429
The headband noticed;

217
00:05:07,429 --> 00:05:08,982
the sleepers mostly didn't.

218
00:05:08,982 --> 00:05:10,502
It was a small study with a

219
00:05:10,502 --> 00:05:11,462
large dose,

220
00:05:11,462 --> 00:05:13,062
so it's not the final word.

221
00:05:13,062 --> 00:05:14,902
But it showed caffeine can take sleep

222
00:05:14,902 --> 00:05:15,622
quietly,

223
00:05:15,622 --> 00:05:17,142
without you feeling robbed.

224
00:05:17,142 --> 00:05:18,582
Which is the most polite kind of

225
00:05:18,582 --> 00:05:19,222
theft.

226
00:05:19,222 --> 00:05:20,822
One study is one study,

227
00:05:20,822 --> 00:05:22,422
so here's the bigger picture.

228
00:05:22,422 --> 00:05:24,102
In 2023,

229
00:05:24,102 --> 00:05:26,502
a team pulled together 24 studies on

230
00:05:26,502 --> 00:05:27,942
caffeine and sleep.

231
00:05:27,942 --> 00:05:28,742
On average,

232
00:05:28,742 --> 00:05:31,222
caffeine cut total sleep by about 45

233
00:05:31,222 --> 00:05:31,862
minutes.

234
00:05:31,862 --> 00:05:33,862
It added roughly nine minutes to falling

235
00:05:33,862 --> 00:05:34,582
asleep,

236
00:05:34,582 --> 00:05:36,342
and about twelve more minutes awake in

237
00:05:36,342 --> 00:05:36,902
the night.

238
00:05:36,902 --> 00:05:38,662
It also changed the kind of sleep

239
00:05:38,662 --> 00:05:39,222
you get.

240
00:05:39,222 --> 00:05:40,982
There was a bit more light sleep,

241
00:05:40,982 --> 00:05:43,222
and about eleven minutes less deep sleep,

242
00:05:43,222 --> 00:05:43,862
the slow,

243
00:05:43,862 --> 00:05:46,019
heavy kind that's hardest to wake from.

244
00:05:46,019 --> 00:05:47,059
From their numbers,

245
00:05:47,059 --> 00:05:49,299
the authors suggested stopping coffee

246
00:05:49,299 --> 00:05:51,859
about nine hours before bed to avoid

247
00:05:51,859 --> 00:05:53,059
losing sleep time.

248
00:05:53,059 --> 00:05:54,179
For a 3 p.m.

249
00:05:54,179 --> 00:05:54,739
cup,

250
00:05:54,739 --> 00:05:56,499
that means bed no earlier than about

251
00:05:56,499 --> 00:05:57,139
midnight.

252
00:05:57,139 --> 00:05:58,739
For a pre-workout powder,

253
00:05:58,739 --> 00:06:00,499
with about twice the caffeine,

254
00:06:00,499 --> 00:06:02,659
they suggested more than thirteen hours.

255
00:06:02,659 --> 00:06:03,699
So a 6 p.m.

256
00:06:03,699 --> 00:06:06,019
gym session would mean a bedtime around

257
00:06:06,019 --> 00:06:07,299
7 the next morning.

258
00:06:07,299 --> 00:06:08,259
It's an average,

259
00:06:08,259 --> 00:06:09,139
not a law,

260
00:06:09,139 --> 00:06:10,979
and some people are far more sensitive

261
00:06:10,979 --> 00:06:11,699
than others.

262
00:06:11,699 --> 00:06:14,019
But it explains why your afternoon coffee

263
00:06:14,019 --> 00:06:15,939
keeps showing up for the night shift.

264
00:06:15,939 --> 00:06:16,179
So,

265
00:06:16,179 --> 00:06:18,019
how does caffeine steal your sleep?

266
00:06:18,019 --> 00:06:19,619
It never gave you energy in the

267
00:06:19,619 --> 00:06:20,256
first place.

268
00:06:20,256 --> 00:06:22,336
It sat in your brain's mailboxes,

269
00:06:22,336 --> 00:06:23,856
wearing a closed sign,

270
00:06:23,856 --> 00:06:25,776
while the tiredness letters piled up

271
00:06:25,776 --> 00:06:26,416
outside.

272
00:06:26,416 --> 00:06:28,016
Your liver cleared it slowly,

273
00:06:28,016 --> 00:06:28,896
half at a time,

274
00:06:28,896 --> 00:06:30,016
over many hours.

275
00:06:30,016 --> 00:06:31,216
And for a 3 p.m.

276
00:06:31,216 --> 00:06:31,936
coffee,

277
00:06:31,936 --> 00:06:33,776
enough can still be there at midnight

278
00:06:33,776 --> 00:06:35,376
to keep a few slots shut.

279
00:06:35,376 --> 00:06:36,576
Not enough to feel.

280
00:06:36,576 --> 00:06:38,336
Just enough to cost you sleep you

281
00:06:38,336 --> 00:06:39,776
might never notice losing.

282
00:06:39,776 --> 00:06:41,296
Like the sleepers in that study,

283
00:06:41,296 --> 00:06:42,896
you'd wake up and swear you slept

284
00:06:42,896 --> 00:06:43,456
fine.

285
00:06:43,456 --> 00:06:45,296
Tiredness doesn't get cancelled;

286
00:06:45,296 --> 00:06:46,656
it gets rescheduled.

287
00:06:46,656 --> 00:06:48,496
None of this makes coffee a villain.

288
00:06:48,496 --> 00:06:50,256
It does exactly what it's built to

289
00:06:50,256 --> 00:06:50,816
do,

290
00:06:50,816 --> 00:06:52,416
just for much longer than the cup

291
00:06:52,416 --> 00:06:53,136
suggests.

292
00:06:53,136 --> 00:06:55,216
The cup and the caffeine simply keep

293
00:06:55,216 --> 00:06:56,496
very different hours.

294
00:06:56,496 --> 00:06:58,256
The mug was empty by four.

295
00:06:58,256 --> 00:06:59,616
The closed sign is still up at

296
00:06:59,616 --> 00:07:00,157
midnight.

297
00:07:00,157 --> 00:07:02,477
But here's the question we didn't answer:

298
00:07:02,477 --> 00:07:05,037
if caffeine only hides your tiredness,

299
00:07:05,037 --> 00:07:06,717
what happens when you drink it every

300
00:07:06,717 --> 00:07:07,597
single day?

301
00:07:07,597 --> 00:07:09,517
That's a mailbox for another day.

302
00:07:09,517 --> 00:07:10,237
Until then,

303
00:07:10,237 --> 00:07:11,677
tell us in the comments:

304
00:07:11,677 --> 00:07:13,919
what time is your last coffee?

305
00:07:15,833 --> 00:07:17,113
Thanks for watching.

306
00:07:17,113 --> 00:07:18,313
If you learned something,

307
00:07:18,313 --> 00:07:19,513
give it a like and share it

308
00:07:19,513 --> 00:07:20,313
with a friend.

309
00:07:20,313 --> 00:07:21,273
And subscribe,

310
00:07:21,273 --> 00:07:23,273
so the next bad idea finds you.

311
00:07:23,273 --> 00:07:25,814
Your next video is right over here.
